WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.870
Disinfectants, hand rub, labels, and growing your business.

2
00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:09.150
This is the video for Module 5 of Liquid Soap, Shampoo and Disinfectants.

3
00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:22.860
Cleaning removes dirt. Disinfecting kills the germs that remain. Dirt shields germs, so always clean first, rinse, then disinfect, and keep the surface wet for the contact time.

4
00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:32.300
To make any strength, use this formula. Bleach strength times bleach volume equals the strength you want times the final volume.

5
00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:48.970
For example, Grace runs cleaning at a clinic in Ilorin. Her bleach is 3.5%. WHO guidance uses 0.1% for general surfaces. For 10 litres, she needs about 286 ml of bleach.

6
00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.740
She adds water to make 10 litres in total.

7
00:00:53.990 --> 00:01:00.820
She labels the bucket with the strength and date, and makes it fresh every day, because diluted bleach loses strength.

8
00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:09.680
Dilute with cool water in a ventilated space, wearing gloves and eye protection. And never mix bleach with any other cleaner.

9
00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:21.280
WHO publishes a guide so health facilities can make hand rub locally. Following it is the safest starting point, because the formula has a recognised basis.

10
00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:29.080
Formulation 1 uses 8,333 ml of 96% ethanol.

11
00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:38.120
417 ml of 3% hydrogen peroxide helps kill spores in the ingredients or bottles.

12
00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:48.750
145 ml of glycerol protects skin. Then top up with clean water to 10 litres. The result is 80% ethanol.

13
00:01:50.000 --> 00:02:02.800
Hold it for 72 hours before use. If your ethanol is weaker, use more. For example, Hadiza's was 90%, so she adjusted her volume to keep the final product at 80%.

14
00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:08.250
Never use methanol. It is poisonous through the skin and if swallowed.

15
00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:16.520
Alcohol vapour catches fire easily. Mix in a ventilated room, away from any flame or generator.

16
00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:25.260
And keep it away from children. Remember, hand rub does not replace washing visibly dirty hands with soap and water.

17
00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:38.350
Until your product is registered, sell cleaning products honestly as cleaners. Training clients to dilute registered disinfectants correctly is a valuable service in its own right.

18
00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:57.670
Words like 'disinfectant' or 'kills 99.9% of germs' are health claims. They need NAFDAC registration and efficacy tests on your actual formula. Never supply unregistered products to clinics, and never call a cleaner a disinfectant to win a tender.

19
00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:07.860
Check every label against this list before printing. Hazardous products like sanitiser and descaler need clear warnings and first aid steps.

20
00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:26.980
Give each bottle a batch code. For example, Ada's code HW-260314-02 means hand wash, made on 14 March 2026, the second batch that day. When a customer complained, she found the batch record in minutes.

21
00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:34.160
Many producers copy market prices and later find packaging and transport eating their profit.

22
00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:39.270
Comfort's ingredients cost about 12,500 naira.

23
00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:47.350
Her packaging costs about 12,000 naira. The pump bottle costs nearly as much as the liquid inside.

24
00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:51.690
Labour and overheads add about 6,000 naira.

25
00:03:52.940 --> 00:04:08.650
The batch costs 30,500 naira, so each bottle costs about 763 naira. She sells wholesale at 1,100 naira, a margin of about 31%. Selling refills can raise that margin.

26
00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:19.840
Hotels, schools and offices reorder every month. For example, Ngozi visits a 40-room hotel in Enugu and leaves samples and dose cards.

27
00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:24.100
The housekeeper records usage for a two-week trial.

28
00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:27.690
Ngozi doubles it to estimate a month.

29
00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:33.760
Her quote comes to about 44,000 naira a month, an illustrative price.

30
00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:50.370
The hotel wants 30-day payment. Her cost is about 70%, so she must fund about 30,800 naira of stock every month before she is paid. She asks for a deposit on the first order, and puts terms in writing.

31
00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:58.020
Now practise your dilutions, plan a hand rub batch on paper, draft three labels, and cost your range.

32
00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:00.310
Continue with the lessons below.
